# Who wrote this, when the commits disagree
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Git records whatever identity a machine was configured with at the time, and
# this repository's history carries three. They are one person.
#
# "Kristina Ziegler <carl@Carls-iMac.local>" is not a second contributor and
# never was: it is the name of the machine the commits were made on, picked up
# as a local Git identity. Fifty-seven commits carry it. Confirmed by the
# project owner.
#
# The commits themselves are left exactly as they are. Rewriting history to
# tidy an identity would change every SHA after the first affected commit,
# including ones already published, deployed and cited - a far worse outcome
# than a mapping file. This is what .mailmap is for: `git shortlog -sne`,
# `git log --use-mailmap` and anything built on them report the canonical
# identity, and the objects are untouched.
#
# Not mapped, deliberately: "gravitas-sim <carlziegler+gravitas@gmail.com>",
# thirteen commits authored through GitHub's web interface. The address looks
# like the owner's, but "looks like" is not confirmation and inventing an
# attribution is the one thing this file must not do. Add a line for it only if
# the owner confirms.
Carl Ziegler <Carl.Ziegler@sfasu.edu> <carl@gravitas-sim.com>
Carl Ziegler <Carl.Ziegler@sfasu.edu> Kristina Ziegler <carl@Carls-iMac.local>
